===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act or result of being uplifted.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2019|month=October|author=Tony Miles and Philip Sherratt|title=EMR kicks off new era|journal=Modern Railways|page=58|text=The EMR Regional timetable improvements also include a significant '''uplift''' in Sunday services.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=2021 February 10|title=Network News: Additional funds enable preparatory work for Ashington reopening|journal=RAIL|issue=924|page=8|text=This means that while initial funding will come from the public purse, landowners along the route will eventually pay back a share of the '''uplift''' in land values created by the new line.}}
# {{lb|en|geology}} A [[tectonic]] [[upheaval]], especially one that takes place in the process of [[mountain]] [[building]].
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1971|author=George Finiel Adams|author2=Jerome Wyckoff|title=Landforms|page=143
|passage=Recent '''uplift''' of the Maine and Oregon coasts has not been enough to "undrown" the larger valleys; the shorelines are still submergent.}}
# {{lb|en|transport}} The [[pick up|picking up]] and [[load]]ing of [[goods]] to be [[transport]]ed by a [[mover]].
# {{lb|en|colloquial}} A [[brassiere]] that raises the [[breast]]s.
# {{lb|en|legal}} An increase in a [[fine]] or [[penalty]] due to aggravating circumstances.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2020|author=David Begg|title=Economics for Business|page=148
|passage=The CMA fined the companies involved £3.4 million, which included '''uplifts''' for director level involvement.}}

